 ## Overview
-Splatt3R is a feed-forward method that predicts 3D Gaussian Splat primitives directly from a pair of uncalibrated natural images, without requiring camera intrinsics, extrinsics, or depth information [S1, unofficial]. It extends the MASt3R foundation model by adding a Gaussian prediction head, enabling both interpolation and extrapolation of novel views in a single forward pass [S1, unofficial]. Unlike existing generalizable 3D-GS methods that are limited to interpolating viewpoints between input stereo views, Splatt3R supervises extrapolated viewpoints beyond the stereo baseline using a novel loss masking strategy to prevent destructive updates from unseen scene regions [S1, unofficial].
+Splatt3R is a feed-forward, pose-free method for 3D reconstruction and novel view synthesis that predicts 3D Gaussian primitives directly from a pair of uncalibrated natural images, without requiring camera intrinsics, extrinsics, or depth maps [S1, unofficial][S2]. It extends the MASt3R foundation model by adding a parallel "Gaussian head" to predict geometric offsets, covariances, opacities, and colors for each pixel [S1, unofficial]. Unlike existing generalizable 3D-GS methods that are limited to interpolating viewpoints between input stereo views, Splatt3R is designed to extrapolate to wider baselines using a novel loss masking strategy that prevents training updates from unseen or obscured scene regions [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** Two uncalibrated RGB images $I_1, I_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 3}$ with resolution $512 \times 512$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Outputs:** A set of 3D Gaussian primitives $\mathcal{G}$, defined by mean position $\mu$, covariance $\Sigma$, opacity $\alpha$, and color $c$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** A pair of uncalibrated RGB images $I_1, I_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 3}$ with resolution $512 \times 512$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A set of 3D Gaussian primitives $\mathcal{G}$, defined by mean positions $\mu$, covariance matrices $\Sigma$ (parameterized by rotation $q$ and scale $s$), opacities $\alpha$, and colors $c$ (or spherical harmonics $S$) [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-The method predicts per-pixel Gaussian parameters. For each pixel $j$, the network outputs:
-*   Base position $x_j \in \mathbb{R}^3$ (from MASt3R backbone).
